# Building, deploying and entering Claude Code

This directory was written by `ansible-playbook aslib.infra.claude_code_podman_setup`. Run every
command below **from this directory**, on a machine with ansible-core and the
aslib.infra collection installed (see aslib.infra's README). Every run writes its
output to a file of its own in `logs/`, such as `logs/deploy-20261003T081502Z.log`.

| File | What it is | Whose | In git |
|---|---|---|---|
| `inventory.yml` | The servers to deploy to | Yours | Yes |
| `group_vars/all/project.yml` | The project's settings: written with every default, then yours | Yours | Yes |
| `container.env` | The container's environment variables and secrets, e.g. an API key | Yours | **No** |
| `../config/` | The policy and Claude's instructions | Yours | Yes |
| `../Containerfile`, `../.containerignore`, `../README.md` | The image, what stays out of its build, running it with plain Podman | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `group_vars/all/defaults.yml` | Every setting at aslib.infra's default; `project.yml` wins | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `image.yml`, `deploy.yml`, `build-and-deploy.yml`, `restart.yml`, `remove.yml` | Build, deploy, both in one go, restart, remove | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `templates/service.container.j2` | The Quadlet file: how Podman and systemd run the service | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `update-playbooks.yml`, `ansible.cfg`, `.gitignore`, this guide | | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `images/`, `logs/`, `*.bak` | The built image, the log of every run, copies an update keeps | | No |

Your files are never overwritten. aslib.infra's are brought up to date by every
update (step 5), which keeps the old version of each changed file as `<file>.bak`:
merge what you need from it, then remove it. At the project's root, a `.bak` is
not git-ignored, so it shows until then.

## 1. Set up

`ansible-playbook aslib.infra.claude_code_podman_setup` wrote this project. Before the first
deploy, look at what is yours: the project's part of the policy in
`../config/managed-settings.json` (see `../README.md`), Claude's instructions in
`../config/CLAUDE.md`, the servers in `inventory.yml` (this machine by default)
and the settings in `group_vars/all/project.yml`.

## 2. Build and deploy

```sh
ansible-playbook build-and-deploy.yml    # add --ask-become-pass if sudo asks for a password
```

This runs `image.yml`, then `deploy.yml`.

**The image** (`image.yml`) needs Podman 4.9 or newer (RHEL 9.6 or Ubuntu 24.04) and
access to the [download sources](#download-sources). It lands in `images/` as
`{{ __claude_code_podman_name }}-<version>.tar`, with the newest version of the
repository's channel unless `claude_code_podman_version` pins one. A build without a
new version, a new base image or a changed Containerfile leaves everything as
it was.

**The deploy** (`deploy.yml`) works as root (through sudo) and, on each server,
runs the image as the always-on service `{{ __claude_code_podman_name }}`, as an account of
its own whose home is `{{ __claude_code_podman_dir }}/`. Claude keeps its login, history,
memory and notes there. `../config/` becomes `config/` in it, read-only for
Claude, who finds it as `/etc/claude-code/`. With `podman_service_shared`, Claude
also works in the shared zone, `/opt/containers/shared/`, which every service of
aslib.infra with that setting may read and write. It installs the command
`{{ __claude_code_podman_name }}` for root.

**How a session starts** comes from the `claude_code_settings_*` settings in
`group_vars/all/project.yml` (or `-e`), the same as in aslib.infra's VM for
Claude Code: by default bypassPermissions mode, without permission prompts
(`claude_code_settings_permission_mode: default` keeps them), Opus at xhigh
effort, the dark theme, agent teams and ultracode off. The policy's deny rules
apply in every mode. Claude Code starts ready to work, without asking to trust a
folder, to confirm the mode or for its first-run setup. With
`claude_code_skills_qa: true`, a request to change a project goes through
aslib.infra's qa skill, which runs five expert subagents one at a time (an
architect, a tester, a reviewer, a developer, a documenter); the deploy puts
them in `config/.claude/` on the server. With
`claude_code_skills_loop_to_delivery: true`, Claude also keeps long work going
by itself until it is delivered, for at most 5 wake-ups and 24 hours
(`claude_code_skills_loop_to_delivery_max_wakes` and `_max_hours`). It needs
`claude_code_settings_qa_budget: true`, whose guard keeps the wake-ups within
the subscription's budget: a deploy with the loop on and qa-budget off stops,
naming both. Its hooks need `/usr/bin/python3`, which the base image provides
(UBI 9 does). Both are off by default, as their names win over skills and
agents of your own.

What a server needs: RHEL 9.6 or newer with Podman, and SSH access with sudo
(or as root) for Ansible.

## 3. Use it

As root on the server:

```sh
sudo -i
{{ __claude_code_podman_name }}                # Claude in the container; arguments go to claude
{{ __claude_code_podman_name }} --continue     # e.g. the last conversation; see {{ __claude_code_podman_name }} --help
```

It runs `claude` in the container as its account, from `{{ __claude_code_podman_dir }}/`, with
your terminal, or without one in a script (`{{ __claude_code_podman_name }} -p "..."`).
Sessions, background sessions and returning to them are Claude Code's own; to
keep a terminal around, use tmux or screen on your side as you like. Claude
signs in with `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` (the token of `claude setup-token`) or
`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in `container.env`; with neither, the first session asks
you to sign in once for everyone: the sign-in page shows a code to paste into
Claude.

A new image, policy or `CLAUDE.md` restarts the service, which ends what runs in
it; the conversations stay, so `{{ __claude_code_podman_name }} --continue` resumes the last one.

## 4. Check on it

```sh
ansible-playbook aslib.infra.overview      # every service, Claude Code included, with how to read its log
```

On the server: `sudo journalctl _SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT={{ __claude_code_podman_name }}.service`.

To restart it, for a change the deploy does not see: `ansible-playbook restart.yml`.
A deploy restarts it by itself when it changes something (the image, `../config/`,
`container.env`, the settings or the Quadlet template). Either way, a restart ends
what runs in the container; conversations stay.

## 5. Update

- **Claude Code:** `ansible-playbook build-and-deploy.yml` builds the newest version
  and deploys it when there is one.
- **aslib.infra:** install the newer release, then `ansible-playbook update-playbooks.yml`.
  It brings aslib.infra's files up to date, keeping each changed one as `<file>.bak`.
  New settings appear in `group_vars/all/defaults.yml`; copy one into `project.yml`
  to change it.
- **The policy or the instructions:** edit `../config/`, then `ansible-playbook deploy.yml`.
  The service restarts with it, as with a new image (step 3).

## 6. Remove it

```sh
ansible-playbook remove.yml                                        # keeps {{ __claude_code_podman_dir }}, with the login
ansible-playbook remove.yml -e podman_service_remove_workdir=true  # removes it as well
```

## Download sources

`image.yml` downloads; `deploy.yml` does not, but installs acl (for the shared
zone) from the server's repositories when it is missing.

| What | Set in the project | Set on the machine that builds |
|---|---|---|
| Red Hat's UBI 9 image, by default from `registry.access.redhat.com` | `claude_code_podman_base_image` | `registries.conf` |
| Claude Code, by default from `downloads.claude.ai` | `claude_code_podman_repository`, `claude_code_podman_key_url` | |
| The tools in the Containerfile | `claude_code_podman_rpm_repos`: a directory of `.repo` files, e.g. a mirror's | Without it, Red Hat's UBI repositories, as set up in the base image; a subscribed RHEL host adds its subscription |
| nvm, by default from `raw.githubusercontent.com` | `claude_code_podman_nvm_url`, `claude_code_podman_nvm_sha256` | |

`claude_code_podman_rpm_repos` is mounted over the base image's `/etc/yum.repos.d` for
the build only: its repositories replace the image's own rather than adding to
them, and the image keeps its own.

A base image other than UBI 9 needs `/usr/bin/python3`: the Containerfile
leaves `python3` to the base image, and the hook of loop-to-delivery runs with
it.

Claude itself needs `api.anthropic.com` and, to sign in, `claude.ai`, `claude.com`
and `platform.claude.com`.
